This is my workaround ATM:

```java
public class AuthenticationFailedExceptionMapper {

    // FIXME: we can do better than this filter
    @RouteFilter(100)
    void myFilter(RoutingContext rc) {
        rc.put(QuarkusHttpUser.AUTH_FAILURE_HANDLER, new BiConsumer<RoutingContext, Throwable>() {
            @Override
            public void accept(RoutingContext routingContext, Throwable throwable) {
                while (throwable.getCause() != null)
                    throwable = throwable.getCause();
                if (throwable instanceof InvalidJwtException) {
                    InvalidJwtException x = (InvalidJwtException) throwable;
                    if (x.hasErrorCode(ErrorCodes.EXPIRED)) {
                        redirectToRoot(routingContext, "Login expired, you've been logged out");
                        return;
                    }
                    redirectToRoot(routingContext, "Invalid session (bad JWT), you've been logged out");
                }
                // This happens when the private/public keys change, like in DEV mode
                if (throwable instanceof AEADBadTagException) {
                    redirectToRoot(routingContext, "Invalid session (bad signature), you've been logged out");
                    return;
                }
                // let upstream handle the rest
            }

            private void redirectToRoot(RoutingContext routingContext, String message) {
                // Note that this calls end()
                routingContext.response().setStatusCode(303);
                routingContext.redirect("/");
            }
        });
        rc.next();
    }
}
```

I seem to be running into this same issue. I am using a custom implementation of HttpAuthenticationMechanism with DynamoDB but no matter what I do, I can't get my `AuthenticationFailedExceptionMapper` to work (see the following):

```java
@Provider
@Priority(Priorities.AUTHENTICATION)
public class AuthenticationFailedExceptionMapper
    implements ExceptionMapper<AuthenticationFailedException> {
  @Override
  public Response toResponse(AuthenticationFailedException e) {
    return Response.status(403).entity(new ErrorResponse("something", "something")).build();
  }
}
```

I've tried using @FroMage 's solution (I've event looked at the [Quarkus Renarde](https://github.com/quarkiverse/quarkus-renarde/tree/15ec74d0897cfa34e8d4c54c08033418eb1c79ec) project to see if there is something I am missing).

I've even attempted the following but to no avail:
* Configured proactive auth (`quarkus.http.auth.proactive=true`)
* I'm currently using `io.quarkus:quarkus-resteasy` and `io.quarkus:quarkus-resteasy-jackson` for my project but I'm not sure if this solution only works with the reactive versions of these libraries (`quarkus-resteasy-reactive` and `quarkus-resteasy-reactive-jackson`)

Could it be due to me using my own custom implementation of `HttpAuthenticationMechanism`? Here is a simplified version that I am expecting to return my custom error response. Perhaps I'm missing something here...

```java
@Alternative
@Priority(1)
@ApplicationScoped
public class BasicAuthAuthenticationMechanism implements HttpAuthenticationMechanism {

  private static final String PREFIX = "BASIC ";

  private static final Logger logger =
      LoggerFactory.getLogger(BasicAuthAuthenticationMechanism.class);

  @Override
  public Uni<SecurityIdentity> authenticate(
      RoutingContext context, IdentityProviderManager identityProviderManager) {
    return Uni.createFrom().failure(new AuthenticationFailedException());
  }

  @Override
  public Uni<ChallengeData> getChallenge(RoutingContext context) {
    return Uni.createFrom().nullItem();
  }

  @Override
  public Set<Class<? extends AuthenticationRequest>> getCredentialTypes() {
    return Set.of(BasicAuthCredentials.class);
  }
}
```
